{"title":"Conclusions","markdown":{"headingText":"Conclusions","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-conclusions","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n## What the application set demonstrates\n\nThirteen applications, spanning fourth- through tenth-order spatial operators in\nthe shipped spectral set, conserved and non-conserved dynamics, dispersive and\ndissipative behaviour, single- and multi-field systems, and both spectral and\nfinite-difference discretisations — on one runtime, with one halo/decomposition\nlayer and matching CPU and GPU paths.\n\nThe two claims from the abstract hold up, with qualifications worth stating.\n\n**High order is cheap to express.** @sec-surface-diffusion ($k^4$),\n@sec-thin-film ($k^4$), @sec-kawahara ($k^5$, imaginary), @sec-ehd-film\n($k^6$) and @sec-higher-order-pfc ($k^{10}$) differ, in their physics headers,\nby the polynomial one function returns. That is a real architectural result and not a presentational trick:\nnone of these applications contains a loop over wave numbers, an MPI call, or a\nhand-written GPU kernel.\n\nThe qualification is that high order is *not* cheap to integrate. The stiffness\ngrows as $\\Delta x^{-n}$, and the reason these models run at useful timesteps\nis @eq-etd1, not the spectral transform by itself. An explicit integrator on\nthe sixth-order operator of @sec-ehd-film would need a timestep smaller by\norders of magnitude.\n\n**The same source scales.** @sec-scalability reports three measured curves out\nto 32 GCDs across four nodes, with checksums agreeing between 1 and 16 GCDs to\nbetween $10^{-12}$ and $3\\times10^{-16}$. The application sources are unchanged\nbetween the one-GCD and 32-GCD runs.\n\nThe qualification is that all three curves fall below 50% efficiency by 24–32\nGCDs at these grid sizes, and the report contains no weak-scaling study, which\nis the measurement that would actually justify a larger simulation.\n\n## The method comparison\n\nThe most useful single result is the pair of Heat3D curves, because they solve\nthe same PDE. Halo exchange strong-scales better than a distributed transpose —\n78% against 59% at one node — for the structural reason that nearest-neighbour\ncommunication stays local as ranks are added while an all-to-all transpose does\nnot.\n\nThis does not make finite differences the better method. The spectral driver is\nunconditionally stable and takes a much larger timestep; the comparison here\nmeasures cost per step, not time to solution. Establishing the latter would\nrequire running both to a fixed accuracy, which has not been done.\n\n## Gaps\n\nHonest accounting of what this report cannot support:\n\n* **Ten of thirteen applications have no scaling measurement.** The spectral\n  ones share a stack with @sec-tungsten and would be expected to track it, but\n  expectation is not measurement.\n* **No CPU control.** Without a LUMI-C run at equal node count, the report\n  cannot quantify what the GPU path buys.\n* **No weak scaling.** Every curve holds the problem fixed.\n* **No time-to-solution comparison** between the spectral and FD paths at\n  matched accuracy — the comparison a user actually needs when choosing.\n* **The two PFC applications have no analytical verification**, only regression\n  and self-consistency. This is inherent to a realistic equation of state, but\n  it means their correctness rests on pinned checksums rather than closed-form\n  agreement.\n\n## Suggested next measurements\n\nIn order of what would most improve this report:\n\n1. **A weak-scaling study** on `tungsten_hip` — fixed cells per GCD, growing\n   grid. This is the curve that says whether a larger science run is feasible,\n   and it is the one currently missing.\n2. **Time-to-solution at matched accuracy** for the two Heat3D drivers, which\n   would turn @sec-scalability's method comparison into actionable guidance.\n3. **A LUMI-C CPU control** at equal node count for one model.\n4. **One scaling point for a second spectral application** — enough to test\n   whether the shared-stack expectation actually holds.\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":"none","code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":false,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":false,"link-external-newwindow":false,"self-contained-math":false,"format-resources":[],"notebook-links":true},"pandoc":{"standalone":true,"wrap":"none","default-image-extension":"png","to":"html","toc":true,"number-sections":true,"html-math-method":"mathjax","output-file":"17_conclusions.html"},"language":{"toc-title-document":"Table of contents","toc-title-website":"On this page","related-formats-title":"Other Formats","related-notebooks-title":"Notebooks","source-notebooks-prefix":"Source","other-links-title":"Other Links","code-links-title":"Code Links","launch-dev-container-title":"Launch Dev Container","launch-binder-title":"Launch Binder","article-notebook-label":"Article Notebook","notebook-preview-down